## 7. Project-Level Fields

Common top-level `project.bbs` fields:

- `id`
- `name`
- `authors`
- `repo`
- `ver`
- `license(...)`
- `configs(...)`
- `filter(...)`
- `targets(...)`
- `builders(...)`
- `find_tool(...)`
- `find_sdk(...)`

Project-level `filter(...)` applies target-style overrides to all targets for one config.

## 8. Config Pattern

When the user wants debug/release behavior, generate this pattern:

```txt
id(my_app)

configs(
  debug
  release
)

targets(
  console(
    output(my_app)
    units(src/main.c)

    filter(
      debug
      defines(DEBUG)
      opt_level(debug)
      warning_level(high)
    )

    filter(
      release
      opt_level(speed_3)
      warnings_as_errors(true)
    )
  )
)
```

Rules:

- `default` always exists even if not listed.
- Config names may contain letters, digits, `_`, `-`, `.`.
- Do not reference undefined config names in `filter(...)`.

## 9. Package Pattern

Package-backed targets are still declared inside `targets(...)`.

Path package:

```txt
static_lib(
  id(my_dep)
  path("../third_party/my_dep")
  cmake_target(my_dep)
)
```

Repo package:

```txt
static_lib(
  id(raylib_pkg)
  repo(
    link("https://github.com/raysan5/raylib.git")
    tag("5.5")
  )
  cmake_target(raylib)
)
```

Archive package:

```txt
static_lib(
  id(my_dep)
  archive(
    link("https://example.com/my_dep.zip")
    strip_prefix("my_dep-1.0.0")
  )
  cmake_target(my_dep)
)
```

Consumer target pattern:

```txt
console(
  output(app)
  dependencies(
    my_dep
  )
  units(src/main.c)
)
```

Package invariants:

- Exactly one source mode per package target: `path`, `repo(...)`, or `archive(...)`.
- Resolved package directory must contain either `CMakeLists.txt` or `project.bbs`.
- If both exist, `bbs` prefers `CMakeLists.txt`.
- `cmake_target(...)` must match the package backend's exported target name.

## 10. Builder Pattern

Use builders only for dynamic command-time logic.

`project.bbs`:

```txt
id(my_app)

targets(
  console(
    id(my_app)
    units(src/main.c)
    dependencies(
      preprocessor
    )
  )
)

builders(
  id(preprocessor)
  units(
    src/builder.c
  )
)
```

Builder source:

```c
#include <bbs/build.h>

bool bbs_callback(bbs_sig signal, bbs_ctx* ctx, bbs_proj* prj, bbs_tgt* tgt) {
  (void)ctx;

  if (signal != BBS_SIG_PRE_BUILD || !prj || !tgt)
    return true;

  if (!bbs_target_has_dependency(tgt, "preprocessor"))
    return true;

  if (!bbs_target_set_text(tgt, BBS_TARGET_TEXT_DEFINES, "PREPROCESSOR_ACTIVE"))
    return false;

  return true;
}
```

Builder facts:

- Entry point must export `bbs_callback(bbs_sig, bbs_ctx*, bbs_proj*, bbs_tgt*)`.
- Builder source includes `#include <bbs/build.h>`.
- Builder ids are referenced from target `dependencies(...)`.
- Builders participate in `build`, `run`, `test`, `dist`, `auto`, `update`.
- Mutations are command-local by default.
- Return `false` to fail the command.

Prefer builder mutations through typed APIs like:

- `bbs_target_set_text(...)`
- `bbs_target_append_text(...)`
- `bbs_project_set_text(...)`
- `bbs_ctx_set_text(...)`

## 11. Dist Pattern

Canonical dist section:

```txt
dist(
  name("$OUT-$CFG-$OS-$ARC")
  archive(true)
  copy_assets(true)
  exclude_assets("dev")
  exclude_assets("raw/*.psd")
  precommand("cp README.md '$DGEN/'")
)
```

Supported `dist(...)` children:

- `name`
- `archive`
- `copy_assets`
- repeated `exclude_assets(...)`
- repeated `precommand(...)`
- repeated `postcommand(...)`

Notes:

- `assetsdir(...)` defaults to `assets` and is created automatically.
- `copy_assets(true)` recursively copies the assets dir into the staged dist payload.
- `exclude_assets(...)` entries are relative to the assets root.

## 12. Token Expansion

Token-aware contexts include hook commands, dist names, `cmake_args`, `cmake_build_args`, `ctest_args`.

Important tokens:

- `$CFG` active config
- `$PLT` platform id, e.g. `windows-x86_64`
- `$OS` OS part
- `$ARC` arch part
- `$TARGET` target id
- `$OUT` target output
- `$VER` resolved version
- `$EXE` absolute executable path when available
- `$DBUILD` resolved build output dir
- `$DASSETS` assets dir
- `$DDIST` resolved dist dir
- `$DGEN` staged dist payload dir
- `$PROJECT(id)` project attribute lookup
- `$CONFIG(path.to.field)` merged config lookup
- `$TOOLCHAIN(path.to.field)` toolchain lookup
- `$$` literal `$`

Example:

```txt
dist_archive_name("$PROJECT(id)-$CFG-$OS-$ARC-$VER")
```

## 13. Commands LLMs Should Emit

Normal workflow:

```bat
bbs build
bbs run
bbs test
bbs dist
```

Useful explicit forms:

```bat
bbs build -t app -c debug -p windows-x86_64
bbs build -t * -p * -c *
bbs run -t app sample.txt
bbs auto -t app --debounce 750
bbs update
bbs update --init-toolchain
bbs update --refresh-packages
bbs package list
bbs package my_dep
bbs package refresh my_dep
bbs gen gitignore
bbs info project
bbs cfg
```

Command facts:

- Working directory should be the project root.
- Platform ids use `<os>-<arch>`.
- `run` only executes host-native outputs.
- Backend initialization is automatic.
- `-t`, `-p`, `-c` can accept `*` on matrix-capable commands.

## 14. Default Layout To Generate

If the user just says “set up a `bbs` project”, prefer:

```txt
project_root/
  project.bbs
  src/
    main.c
  include/
  assets/
```

Notes:

- `assets/` is a good default because `bbs` expects it conceptually and can copy it into dist payloads.
- `build/` and `dist/` are generated; do not hand-author them.
- Local `config.bbs` is optional and usually not committed.

## 15. Canonical Recipes

Single executable:

```txt
id(app)
name("App")
ver(0.1.0)

targets(
  console(
    output(app)
    units(src/main.c)
  )
)
```

Static library plus app:

```txt
id(example)

targets(
  static_lib(
    id(core)
    include_dirs(src)
    units(src/core.c)
  )

  console(
    output(example)
    include_dirs(src)
    dependencies(
      core
    )
    units(src/main.c)
  )
)
```

Test target:

```txt
targets(
  static_lib(
    id(core)
    units(src/core.c)
  )

  test(
    output(core_tests)
    dependencies(
      core
    )
    units(tests/test_main.c)
  )
)
```

## 16. Error-Avoidance Rules

Do not do these:

- Do not assume users must write `CMakeLists.txt` for ordinary local targets.
- Do not invent JSON, YAML, or TOML syntax; `bbs` uses `.bbs` tree syntax.
- Do not use commas as if they matter.
- Do not use single quotes for `.bbs` strings.
- Do not place `repo(...)` as a top-level project metadata section when defining a package target; package `repo(...)` belongs inside a target.
- Do not declare multiple package source modes on one target.
- Do not use raw system libraries in `dependencies(...)`; use `link_libs(...)` for those.
- Do not expect `bbs run` to execute cross-built binaries locally.
- Do not use builders for static always-on settings that fit directly in target fields.
- Do not invent target types beyond the supported list.
- Do not assume hook commands are CMD/PowerShell; docs define them as Bash commands.

## 17. Best-Guess Defaults

If the prompt is underspecified, choose:

- target type: `console`
- language: `c`
- version: `0.1.0`
- source path: `src/main.c`
- output name: same as project `id`
- config list: omit unless debug/release behavior is requested
- runtime: omit unless static runtime behavior is explicitly needed
- warnings/optimization: omit unless requested
- dist: omit unless packaging/distribution is requested
- builders: omit unless dynamic metacompilation is requested

## 18. Fast Decision Table

- Want an app binary -> `console(...)`
- Want a GUI/no-console binary -> `consoleless(...)`
- Want reusable compiled code -> `static_lib(...)`
- Want tests -> `test(...)`
- Want config-specific flags -> `configs(...)` + `filter(...)`
- Want external dependency -> package-backed target + `dependencies(...)`
- Want dynamic prebuild mutation -> `builders(...)`
- Want staged release payload/archive -> `dist(...)` + `bbs dist`
- Want generated ignore/workflow file -> `bbs gen ...`

## 19. LLM Output Template

When generating a new `bbs` project, emit this shape unless task constraints require more:

```txt
id(<project_id>)
name("<Project Name>")
ver(0.1.0)

targets(
  console(
    output(<project_id>)
    units(
      src/main.c
    )
  )
)
```

Then instruct execution with:

```bat
bbs build
bbs run
```

## 20. Validation Checklist

Before considering generated `bbs` content done, verify mentally:

1. `project.bbs` exists in project root.
2. There is at least one target inside `targets(...)`.
3. Every target uses a supported target type.
4. Every local target has `units(...)` unless it is package-backed or header-only.
5. `dependencies(...)` names `bbs` targets or builder ids, not system libs.
6. Package targets define exactly one of `path`, `repo(...)`, `archive(...)`.
7. Package targets include `cmake_target(...)`.
8. All config names used in `filter(...)` are valid.
9. Hook strings that need spaces use double-quoted `.bbs` strings.
10. Suggested commands are valid `bbs` commands.
